:title("C# (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses a C# subset and executes it directly, without generating code.

The subset: 'using' directives (resolved against the runtime builtins, like imports),
'namespace X.Y { ... }' blocks (unwrapped; declarations inside behave like top level
ones), classes with instance and static fields (with initializers), instance and static
methods (block bodied and expression bodied '=> e;'), constructors (with an optional
': base(...)' / ': this(...)' header that is parsed and skipped), single inheritance
('class B : A' inherits fields and methods, overrides with dynamic dispatch, runs the
implicit base constructor, and 'base.M()' calls; a base class must appear before its
subclasses), object creation with 'new', arrays (new int[n], new int[]{...}, a.Length,
indexing), List<T> (new List<T>(), .Add, [i], .Count, .Contains, foreach), Dictionary<K,V>
(new Dictionary<K,V>(), d[k] get/set, .Count, .ContainsKey, .Keys, .Values), the 'foreach
(var x in coll)' loop, 'var' and typed locals/params/returns (types are parsed; the
semantics are dynamic), string concatenation with +, '$\"...{expr}...\"' interpolation,
the String members .Length, .Substring, .IndexOf and .Equals, Console.WriteLine /
Console.Write mapped to the print builtin, if/else, while, do-while, both for forms, the
colon switch on int or string (fallthrough with break, stacked labels, default),
break/continue, return, assignment (also += -= *= /= %=), postfix ++/--, the ternary
operator, || && with short circuit, == != < > <= >= (== compares objects by identity and
strings by value) and + - * / % (int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates) and unary - !.

Also modelled: char as an integral value that renders as its glyph (with the C# escapes
and the (char)/(int)/(byte) conversions), the full numeric literal set (leading-dot reals,
hex, binary, digit groups, u/l/f/d/m suffixes), verbatim and raw string literals,
interpolation with alignment and the D format specifier, typeof / nameof / sizeof /
default, nullable values with the null-coalescing, null-conditional and null-forgiving
operators and .HasValue / .Value, nested types, enums (with member initializers over
earlier members), structs (value copy on a struct-typed declaration, and a zeroed
default), interfaces with default members and explicit implementations, abstract members,
extension methods (a static method whose first parameter carries this), unqualified calls
to members of the enclosing class, using statements and using declarations with Dispose in
reverse order, local functions, lambdas with typed parameters, default values and a params
tail, anonymous methods and anonymous types, braced array initializers, and object
creation as the head of a member chain.

Also modelled: labels and goto (including goto case / goto default, which re-enter the
enclosing switch), the throw EXPRESSION, the BCL exception types (a program may throw new
Exception(...) without declaring one), named and optional arguments (a skipped parameter
takes its default), collection expressions with a spread element, the index-from-end
operator and range slicing, delegate { ... } without a parameter list, unsafe blocks,
implicitly typed and sized-with-initializer arrays, nested and indexed member
initializers, and explicitly implemented properties.

Also modelled: tuples (a tuple type, positional and named elements) and deconstruction
('var (a, b) = e', '(a, b) = e', a custom Deconstruct(out ...) and a record's positional
members); the pattern grammar (constant, relational, type and declaration, var, discard,
property, positional and list patterns, combined with and / or / not) behind 'is', behind
the switch EXPRESSION and behind the case labels of the switch STATEMENT, both with their
'when' guards; records ('record R(int X);', 'record class', 'record struct') with
deconstruction and the 'with' expression; delegates and events (method group conversion,
anonymous methods, multicast '+=', and '-=' removing the last matching invocation, exactly
as Delegate.Remove does); and LAZY iterator blocks ('yield return' / 'yield break') with
foreach, GetEnumerator / MoveNext / Current - an endless iterator that a foreach breaks
out of terminates.

Records and tuples compare BY VALUE, member by member and recursively, so a record whose
field is itself a record or a tuple compares by value too; every other class keeps
reference equality, and a user-declared 'operator ==' still wins over both.

Also modelled: an event that manages its own subscription list
('event H E { add { ... } remove { ... } }'), whose accessors 'E += h' / 'E -= h' call with
the handler itself.

Widened surface (parsed so a whole C# file is accepted): generics, nullable and array type
suffixes are parsed and dropped; modifier keywords are parsed and ignored. A delegate type
declaration is accepted and dropped (a delegate value is the closure it names); so are a
finalizer ('~X() { }' - nothing here collects), 'extern alias X;' and the 'X::Name' alias
qualifier it enables. LINQ query expressions are accepted and reported as not implemented;
-warn-unsupported downgrades that to a warning so call graphs / CFGs / traces still work on
partly understood files.

An iterator is driven by REPLAY: there are no coroutines here, so every step re-runs the
body from the top, skips the yields already delivered and stops at the next one. A pure
iterator behaves exactly right; one with side effects repeats them (the model
languages/python-interpreter.abnf uses; the compiled twin has real generators instead).

Not modelled exactly: async members run synchronously (await is the identity on its
operand), which is a documented approximation rather than a gap - the async machinery has
no scheduler here. A bare type-name case label ('case IShape:', with no binding) is read as
a constant, because telling a type from a constant needs name resolution; the binding form
('case IShape s:') and every built-in type name are read as patterns. Out and ref
parameters are ordinary locals, read back only by a deconstruction.

Not genuinely modelled: method overloading, unqualified FIELD access (fields are reached
via this.x or ClassName.x; only an unqualified CALL resolves against the enclosing class),
value semantics for structs outside a struct-typed declaration, disposal on a path that
leaves a using block by an exception, and LINQ.

Fields live directly on the instance object, methods on the class descriptor; a
constructor always exists (a default one is generated) and first applies the field
initializers. The static Main of class Program is the entry point; the process exit code
is Main's return value, so self checking programs 'return failures;'.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     = { Using } { TopDecl } ;

// A 'using' directive (also 'using static' and the 'using Alias = Path;' form) is
// resolved against the runtime builtins (core.stdlibImports); one that does not resolve
// aborts, unless -warn-imports downgrades it to a warning (see resolveImport). It leaves
// no value on the stack.
Using       <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = [ "global" KwEnd ] KwUsing [ "static" KwEnd ] [ Id "=" ] QualName ";" ;
QualName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Id [ "::" Id ] { "." Id } [ "." "*" ] ;

TopDecl     = ExternAlias | TopAttr | Namespace | FileNs | UnsupType | TypeDecl ;
// 'extern alias X;': an assembly alias. There are no assemblies here, so it is parsed and
// dropped; the 'X::Name' references it enables are read as plain qualified names.
ExternAlias = "extern" KwEnd "alias" KwEnd Id ";" ;
// The type declarations that are genuinely modelled.
TypeDecl    = ClassDecl | EnumDecl ;

// A standalone attribute section at file scope ('[assembly: ...]' / '[module: ...]'):
// accepted and ignored (it pushes no declaration).
TopAttr     = AttrSection ;
// A file scoped namespace ('namespace X.Y;'): a no-op marker; the declarations that
// follow it register themselves at top level (like a block namespace, which is unwrapped).
// The name QualName pushes is discarded so it never lands in a later declaration.
FileNs      <~~ pop() ~~> = KwNamespace QualName ";" ;

// namespace X.Y { ... }: unwrapped. The inner declarations register themselves
// (their tags run during the match); the namespace tag just clears its own name.
Namespace   <~~ takeAll() ~~>
            = KwNamespace QualName "{" { Using | TopDecl } "}" ;

ClassDecl   <~~ buildClass(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ ClassMods ] ClassKw ClassName [ ClassTPs ] [ PrimaryCtor ]
              [ ":" BaseSpec [ SkipParen ] { "," Type [ SkipParen ] } ] { WhereClause } ClassBody ;
// A record may stand without a body ('record R(int X);').
ClassBody   = "{" { Member } "}" | ";" ;
// The modifiers before a type declaration. Only 'partial' is behavioral: the members of
// every part of a partial class are merged into one class (see buildClass).
ClassMods   <~~ push({cmods: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = LeadMods ;
// The type-parameter names of a generic type ('class Pair<TA, TB>'). They are kept so a
// 'new TB()' inside the body can be resolved through the type arguments the instance was
// created with (see makeNew / csResolveTypeName).
ClassTPs    <~~ push({tparams: csTypeArgs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = SkipAngle ;
// A primary constructor ('class C(int n) { ... }'): the parameters become instance
// members of the class, so the class body may name them unqualified.
PrimaryCtor <~~ push({primary: pop()}) ~~>
            = ParamList ;
// The first base type. A qualified name ('B.Inner') keeps its last segment, which is the
// simple name every type is registered under.
BaseSpec    <~~ push({ext: csLastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
            = Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
// 'class' or 'struct': a struct is built exactly like a class, and additionally copies
// on a declaration whose declared type names it (C# value semantics) and has a zeroed
// default instance.
ClassKw     <~~ push({ckind: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = RecordKw | ( "class" | "struct" | "interface" ) KwEnd ;
// A record ('record R(int X);', 'record class', 'record struct'). It is built exactly
// like the class / struct it names: the positional parameters are a primary constructor,
// so they already become members. What a record adds is value equality, deconstruction
// from those parameters, and the 'with' expression - see buildClassFrom.
RecordKw    = "record" KwEnd [ ( "class" | "struct" ) KwEnd ] ;
// The class name, and the class the enclosing member bodies belong to (curClassName, for
// 'base.M()'): pushed on a stack so a nested class restores its outer name when it ends.
ClassName   <~~ push(up.in); classStack.push(curClassName); curClassName = up.in ~~>
            = Id ;

Member      = NestedType | NestedUnsup | DelegateDecl | OperatorDecl | Finalizer | Ctor
            | Method | AbstractMethod | IndexerDecl | Property | Field ;

// A finalizer ('~X() { ... }'). There is no garbage collector to run it, so it is parsed
// and dropped - it pushes nothing, and the class body simply skips it.
Finalizer   = [ LeadMods ] "~" Id ParamList ( Block | ";" ) ;

// An overloaded operator ('public static Vec operator +(Vec a, Vec b)', also the unary
// forms) and a user conversion ('public static implicit operator Vec(int n)'). Both are
// static members of the declaring type; they become '__op1_<sym>' / '__op2_<sym>' and
// '__conv_<T>' members, which binOp / makeNeg / makeCast / a typed declaration consult.
OperatorDecl = ConvDecl | BinOpDecl ;
ConvDecl    <~~ push(makeConvOp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods ConvKind KwOperator CType ParamList MethodBody ;
ConvKind    = ( "implicit" | "explicit" ) KwEnd ;
CType       <~~ push({cty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
BinOpDecl   <~~ push(makeOperatorDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType KwOperator OpSym ParamList MethodBody ;
// Longer symbols first, so '++' is never read as '+'.
OpSym       <~~ push({opsym: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = "<<" | ">>" | "==" | "!=" | "<=" | ">=" | "++" | "--" | "true" KwEnd
            | "false" KwEnd | "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "%" | "&" | "|" | "^" | "~" | "!"
            | "<" | ">" ;
KwOperator  = "operator" KwEnd ;

// An indexer ('public int this[int i] { get { ... } }'): its accessors become the
// class' __index / __setindex members, which the index read and the index assignment
// consult when the receiver is an instance of a class that declares one.
IndexerDecl <~~ push(makeIndexer(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType KwThis IdxParams PropAccs ;
IdxParams   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ Param { "," Param } ] "]" ;

// A type declared inside a class body is a real type: it registers under its simple
// name like a top level one, and the outer class descriptor gets a member of that name so
// 'Outer.Inner' resolves too.
NestedType  <~~ push(makeNested()) ~~>
            = TypeDecl ;

// A constructor: name matches the class; an optional ': base(...)' / ': this(...)'
// header is parsed and skipped (the implicit base constructor already ran). The body is
// a block or an expression bodied '=> e;' form.
Ctor        <~~ push(makeCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ CtorInit ] CtorBody ;
CtorInit    = ":" ( KwBase | KwThis ) SkipParen ;
CtorBody    = Block | CtorArrow ;
CtorArrow   <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~> = "=>" Expression ";" ;

// A method. The name may be qualified with the interface it explicitly implements
// ('string ITag.Tag()'); only the last segment is kept, so the member is reachable under
// its plain name (the interface view is not modelled separately).
Method      <~~ push(makeMethod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods Type MethodName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList { WhereClause } MethodBody ;
// csSimpleName drops the type arguments of both the interface and the method itself
// ('IEnumerable<Foo>.GetEnumerator', 'Echo<T>') and keeps the last segment.
MethodName  <~~ push(csSimpleName(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
MethodBody  = Block | ArrowBody ;
// A member declared without a body: an abstract method, or an interface method that has
// no default implementation. It contributes no member, so a subclass' override is what
// the dispatch finds.
AbstractMethod <~~ push(makeAbstract(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods Type MethodName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList { WhereClause } ";" ;
ArrowBody   <~~ push(makeArrowBody(pop())) ~~>
            = "=>" Expression ";" ;

// A property. An automatically implemented accessor pair ('{ get; set; }', 'init' and
// 'required' included) makes the property a plain FIELD of the class - so an object
// initializer, a read and a write all work unchanged. An accessor with a body instead
// becomes a '__get_<name>' / '__set_<name>' member, which the field read (core.getField)
// and the assignment path consult when the object carries no member of that name.
Property    <~~ push(makeProperty(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType PropName ( PropAccs [ PropInit ] | PropArrow ) ;
// The name may be qualified with the interface it explicitly implements
// ('int I.P { get { ... } }'); only the last segment is kept.
PropName    <~~ push({pname: csSimpleName(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
PropAccs    <~~ push({accs: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { Accessor } "}" ;
Accessor    = GetAcc | SetAcc | AddAcc | RemoveAcc ;
GetAcc      <~~ push(makeAccessor("get", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods "get" KwEnd AccBody ;
SetAcc      <~~ push(makeAccessor("set", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods ( "set" | "init" ) KwEnd AccBody ;
// An accessor without a body ('get;') is the automatic one.
// The accessors of an EVENT that manages its own subscription list
// ('event EventHandler E { add { ... } remove { ... } }'). They become '__add_E' /
// '__remove_E' members, which 'E += h' / 'E -= h' call with the handler.
AddAcc      <~~ push(makeAccessor("add", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods "add" KwEnd AccBody ;
RemoveAcc   <~~ push(makeAccessor("remove", takeAll())) ~~>
            = LeadMods "remove" KwEnd AccBody ;
AccBody     = ";" | Block | ArrowBody ;
PropInit    <~~ push({pinit: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" InitVal ";" ;
PropArrow   <~~ push({parrow: pop()}) ~~>
            = ArrowBody ;

// A field declaration may declare SEVERAL names ('public int X, Y;'). Each declarator
// becomes a field of its own; buildClass flattens the group before the members are read.
Field       <~~ push(makeFieldGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods FType FieldDecl { "," FieldDecl } ";" ;
FieldDecl   <~~ push({fdecl: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "=" InitVal ] ;
// An initializer value. The braced array initializer ('int[] a = {1, 2};') is only legal
// here and in a local declaration, so it is not part of Expression.
InitVal     = ArrLit | Expression ;
ArrLit      <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
ArrElem     = ArrLit | Expression ;

// Modifiers before a member; ModWord keeps the text so 'static' stays detectable. An
// attribute section here (e.g. '[Obsolete] static int F') is accepted and dropped.
Mods        <~~ push({mods: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = { ModWord | AttrSection } ;
ModWord     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = ModKw ;
FType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = Type ;

// A parameter list. 'this' on the first parameter marks an extension method and 'params'
// marks the variadic tail, so both modifiers leave a marker; the rest are dropped.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
Param       = { AttrSection } { ParamMod } Type [ "..." ] ParamName [ ParamDflt ] ;
ParamName   <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
ParamDflt   <~~ push({pdflt: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" Expression ;
ParamMod    = ThisMod | ParamsMod | ( "ref" | "out" | "in" | "readonly" | "scoped" ) KwEnd ;
ThisMod     <~~ push({thisParam: true}) ~~>
            = "this" KwEnd ;
ParamsMod   <~~ push({paramsParam: true}) ~~>
            = "params" KwEnd ;

// Types are parsed and (apart from field defaults) ignored: generic arguments (<...>),
// dotted names, array suffixes and the nullable '?' are accepted and dropped.
// The pointer suffix ('int*', 'S**') is accepted and dropped: unsafe code parses, and a
// pointer behaves like the reference it names here.
Type        = TypeName { "*" | "[" { "," } "]" } [ "?" ] ;
TypeName    = TupleType | PrimType | "string" KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "object" KwEnd | "void" KwEnd
            | "var" KwEnd | "dynamic" KwEnd | UserType ;
// A tuple type ('(int, int)', '(int lo, int hi)'). It needs at least two elements, so a
// parenthesized type is never read as one. The element names are part of the TYPE only;
// the value carries the names its literal spells out (see makeTuple).
TupleType   = "(" TupTyElem "," TupTyElem { "," TupTyElem } ")" ;
TupTyElem   = Type [ Id ] ;
PrimType    = ( "int" | "uint" | "long" | "ulong" | "short" | "ushort" | "byte" | "sbyte"
              | "double" | "float" | "decimal" | "bool" | "char" ) KwEnd ;
// 'A::B.C' - the namespace-alias qualifier of an 'extern alias' - reads like a dotted
// name; only the last segment is kept anywhere, so the alias itself never matters.
UserType    = Id [ "::" Id ] [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue
            | Goto | Try | Throw | UsingStmt | Lock | Checked | LocalFunc | YieldStmt
            | Decon | Decl | Empty | ExprStmt ;

// 'goto L;' travels as a {sig:"goto"} signal until a block that declares L catches it
// and resumes there (the same shape go-interpreter.abnf uses); 'goto case c;' and
// 'goto default;' re-dispatch the enclosing switch instead. The longer forms are
// listed first, so 'goto default' is never read as a jump to a label named 'default'.
Goto        = GotoCase | GotoDflt | GotoLabel ;
GotoCase    <~~ push(makeGotoCase(pop())) ~~>
            = KwGoto KwCase CondExpr ";" ;
GotoDflt    <~~ push(makeGotoDefault()) ~~>
            = KwGoto KwDefault ";" ;
GotoLabel   <~~ push(makeGoto(pop())) ~~>
            = KwGoto LabelName ";" ;
LabelName   <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;

// A local function: a named closure over the enclosing locals, declared like a method.
// It is bound before its body can run, so it may call itself.
LocalFunc   <~~ push(makeLocalFunc(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] ParamList MethodBody ;

// 'checked { ... }' / 'unchecked { ... }': the block runs (overflow checking is not
// modelled); the keyword is dropped and the inner block thunk stands in for it.
Checked     = ( "checked" KwEnd | "unchecked" KwEnd | "unsafe" KwEnd ) Block ;

// 'lock (e) stmt': the monitor is not modelled; the guard expression still runs (so its
// calls stay visible) and the guarded statement executes.
Lock        <~~ push(makeLock(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = "lock" KwEnd "(" Expression ")" Statement ;

// 'using (init) stmt': the resource is created, the body runs, then Dispose() is called
// on it - and on several resources in reverse order. The 'using var r = e;' declaration
// form is a BlockItem rather than a Statement (it scopes the rest of its block, which is
// exactly the using statement C# rewrites it to - see makeBlockStmt).
UsingStmt   <~~ push(makeUsing(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwUsing "(" UsingInit ")" Statement ;
UsingInit   = UsingDecl | UsingExpr ;
// 'using (new Res())' is an expression, but 'new Res' also reads as a type plus a
// declarator - so a declaration here must not be followed by an argument list.
UsingDecl   <~~ push(makeUseDecl(pop())) ~~>
            = DeclCore !"(" ;
UsingExpr   <~~ push({useExpr: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;

// A block. 'using var r = e;' is a BlockItem of its own (not a Statement), so
// makeBlockStmt sees a marker record and can scope the rest of the block under it.
Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { BlockItem } "}" ;
BlockItem   = UsingItem | LabelItem | Statement ;
UsingItem   <~~ push({usingItem: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwUsing UsingDecl ";" ;
// A label ('done:'). It is a BlockItem of its own rather than a prefix of the statement
// it marks, so a label may also stand at the very end of a block. The '!":"' keeps the
// namespace alias qualifier 'A::B' out.
LabelItem   <~~ push({labelName: pop()}) ~~>
            = LabelName ":" !":" ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" Statement [ KwElse Statement ] ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Statement ;

Do          <~~ push(makeDo(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwDo Statement KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ";" ;

// The colon switch (on int or string): starts at the first matching label and runs
// until a break or the end of the switch. Labels may stack.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchCase } "}" ;
SwitchCase  = Case | Default ;
// A case label is a PATTERN in C# 7 and later ('case int n when n > 0:', 'case > 5:',
// 'case string s:'). The constant form is tried FIRST, because an enum member
// ('case Color.Red:') reads as a type pattern otherwise and a PEG never goes back - and
// because only a constant label is a target for 'goto case c;'.
Case        = TypeKwCase | ConstCase | PatCase ;
// 'case string:' / 'case int n:'. A bare built-in type name can never begin a constant
// expression, so it has to be read as a type pattern before ConstCase gets to it - while
// 'case int.MaxValue:' still falls through, because the '.' defeats this rule.
TypeKwCase  <~~ push(makePatCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase TypeKwPat [ ArmGuard ] ":" { Statement } ;
TypeKwPat   <~~ push(csPatType(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TypeKw [ PatBind ] ;
TypeKw      <~~ push({patty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( PrimType | "string" KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "object" KwEnd | "dynamic" KwEnd )
              { "*" | "[" { "," } "]" } [ "?" ] ;
ConstCase   <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase CondExpr ":" { Statement } ;
PatCase     <~~ push(makePatCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase Pattern [ ArmGuard ] ":" { Statement } ;
Default     <~~ push(makeDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDefault ":" { Statement } ;

// foreach (var x in coll) body.
ForEach     <~~ push(makeForEach(pop(), pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwForeach "(" Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> KwIn Expression ")" Statement ;

For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" [ ForInit ] ";" [ ForCond ] ";" [ ForPost ] ")" Statement ;
ForInit     <~~ push({k:"init", t:pop()}) ~~> = ForVar | ForExpr ;
ForVar      = DeclCore ;
ForExpr     <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~> = Expression ;
ForCond     <~~ push({k:"cond", t:pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForPost     <~~ push({k:"post", t:exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] ";" ;

// An iterator block. A method whose body contains a 'yield' produces a SEQUENCE: the
// body runs once with a hidden accumulator, every 'yield return e' appends to it and
// 'yield break' ends it early, and the accumulator is what the call answers. The sequence
// is therefore materialized eagerly - an endless iterator does not terminate here - which
// is what makes 'foreach', LINQ-free enumeration and GetEnumerator/MoveNext/Current all
// work on the ordinary array representation.
YieldStmt   = YieldBreak | YieldReturn ;
YieldReturn <~~ push(makeYieldReturn(pop())) ~~>
            = KwYield KwReturn Expression ";" ;
YieldBreak  <~~ push(makeYieldBreak()) ~~>
            = KwYield KwBreak ";" ;
KwYield     = "yield" KwEnd ;

Break       <~~ push(makeBreak()) ~~>    = KwBreak ";" ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue()) ~~> = KwContinue ";" ;

// try/catch/finally and throw are implemented (excThrow/excTry, languages/lib): throw
// raises the value, catch binds it (the first catch clause wins - the exception type and
// an optional 'when (cond)' filter are parsed but not discriminated / evaluated), finally
// always runs. return/break/continue inside a try still propagate. A catch may omit the
// parens ('catch { }'), the binding ('catch (T) { }') or bind a name ('catch (T e) { }').
Try         <~~ push(excTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(excCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch [ "(" Type [ Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ")" ] [ KwWhen "(" DiscardExpr ")" ] Block ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
// throw e; raises e; a bare throw; (rethrow) has no value here, so it raises undefined.
Throw       <~~ push(excThrow(csThrowVal(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = KwThrow [ Expression ] ";" ;

// A deconstruction statement: 'var (a, b) = e;' declares both names, '(a, b) = e;' assigns
// to names that already exist, and a typed element ('(int q, string s) = e') declares that
// one. The right side may be a tuple, or any object with a Deconstruct(out ...) method -
// see csDeconstruct. Two elements at least, so '(x) = e' is never read as one.
Decon       <~~ push(makeDecon(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ DeconVar ] "(" DeconTgt "," DeconTgt { "," DeconTgt } ")" "=" Expression ";" ;
DeconVar    <~~ push({deconVar: true}) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd ;
DeconTgt    = DeconTyped | DeconPlain ;
DeconTyped  <~~ push({dtName: pop(), dtDecl: true}) ~~>
            = Type DeconId ;
DeconPlain  <~~ push({dtName: pop(), dtDecl: false}) ~~>
            = DeconId ;
DeconId     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;

// A local declaration. The declared type reaches the declarators (DType), so a variable
// without an initializer - and a bare '= default' - gets that type's default value.
Decl        = DeclCore ";" ;
DeclCore    <~~ push(makeDeclSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { LocalMod } DType Declarator { "," Declarator } ;
// Modifiers a LOCAL declaration may carry. They are parsed and dropped: 'const' and
// 'readonly' are compile-time promises, and 'ref' / 'scoped' locals alias rather than
// copy, which this model does not distinguish.
LocalMod    = ( "const" | "readonly" | "static" | "volatile" | "unsafe" | "ref"
              | "scoped" | "fixed" ) KwEnd ;
DType       <~~ push({dty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
Declarator  <~~ push(makeDeclInfo(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ DeclInit ] ;
DeclInit    = DeclDefault | DeclExpr ;
// '= default' without a type argument takes the declared type's default.
DeclDefault <~~ push({bareDefault: true}) ~~>
            = "=" "default" KwEnd !"(" ;
DeclExpr    <~~ push({dinit: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" InitVal ;

Empty       <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression ";" ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value.
// A throw EXPRESSION ('n > 0 ? n : throw new Exception("neg")') raises instead of
// producing a value, so it may stand wherever a value is expected; its thunk is the
// same one the throw STATEMENT uses.
Expression  = Assignment | ThrowExpr | CondExpr ;
ThrowExpr   <~~ push(excThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow CondExpr ;
// The compound bitwise/shift forms apply like their arithmetic siblings; '??=' is
// accepted but reported as not implemented (it needs a null test). Longer operators are
// listed before their prefixes.
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Target ( "<<=" | ">>=" | "??=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%="
                     | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | ( "=" !"=" ) ) <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> Expression ;

// An assignable place: a variable (this and class names are variables too), or a
// member/index path: this.x, p.X, Program.Fails, arr[i], dict[k].
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression { "," DiscardExpr } "]" ;

CondExpr    <~~ push(makeCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NullCoal [ "?" Expression ":" Expression ] ;

// Null-coalescing 'a ?? b': the first operand that is not null wins.
NullCoal    <~~ push(makeNullCoal(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = OrExpr { "??" ( ThrowExpr | OrExpr ) } ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;

AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = BitOr { "&&" BitOr } ;

// The bitwise levels sit between && and equality (as in C#). A lone '|' that actually
// begins '||' fails its right side, the iteration rolls back, and the '||' level above
// picks it up (likewise '&' vs '&&').
BitOr       <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitXor { "|" <~~ push("|") ~~> BitXor } ;
BitXor      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitAnd { "^" <~~ push("^") ~~> BitAnd } ;
BitAnd      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&" <~~ push("&") ~~> EqExpr } ;

EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "==" | "!=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;

RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TypeTest { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> TypeTest } ;

// 'e is Pattern' / 'e as Type'. The patterns modelled are the constant ones (null, true,
// false) and the type pattern, with an optional binding ('o is string s') and an optional
// 'not'. 'e as T' yields e when it is a T and null otherwise. csIsType is the runtime type
// test; a binding declares its name in the enclosing scope, as C# does.
TypeTest    <~~ push(makeTypeTest(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Shift [ IsAs | SwitchExpr ] ;
IsAs        = AsTest | IsTest ;
AsTest      <~~ push({asty: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwAs TypeArg ;
IsTest      <~~ push({ispat: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwIs Pattern ;

// The switch EXPRESSION ('n switch { > 8 => "A", _ => "C" }'): the first arm whose
// pattern matches - and whose optional 'when' guard holds - supplies the value. It is a
// suffix of the shift level, so 'e switch {...} == x' still compares the result.
SwitchExpr  <~~ push({swarms: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "{" [ SwitchArm { "," SwitchArm } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
SwitchArm   <~~ push(csSwitchArm(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Pattern [ ArmGuard ] "=>" Expression ;
ArmGuard    <~~ push({guard: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwWhen Expression ;

// ----- patterns -----
// The C# pattern grammar: constant, relational, type / declaration, var, discard,
// property, positional and list patterns, combined with 'and' / 'or' / 'not'. Every
// production pushes exactly one pattern record; the matcher walks that record.
Pattern     = OrPat ;
OrPat       <~~ push(csPatOr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AndPat { KwOr AndPat } ;
AndPat      <~~ push(csPatAnd(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NotPat { KwAnd NotPat } ;
NotPat      = NotForm | PrimPat ;
NotForm     <~~ push(csPatNot(pop())) ~~>
            = KwNot NotPat ;
// Ordered longest-first: a property pattern and a positional pattern both BEGIN with a
// type name, so the bare type pattern has to be tried after both of them - a PEG never
// goes back. The constant pattern is last, because a type name reads as an expression too.
PrimPat     = RelPat | NullPat | TruePat | FalsePat | DiscardPat | VarPat | ListPat
            | PosPatT | PropPat | PosPat | ParenPat | TypePat | ConstPat ;
RelPat      <~~ push(csPatRel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> Shift ;
NullPat     <~~ push(csPatNull()) ~~>
            = "null" KwEnd ;
TruePat     <~~ push(csPatConst(makeConst(true))) ~~>
            = "true" KwEnd ;
FalsePat    <~~ push(csPatConst(makeConst(false))) ~~>
            = "false" KwEnd ;
DiscardPat  <~~ push(csPatDiscard()) ~~>
            = "_" KwEnd ;
VarPat      <~~ push(csPatVar(pop())) ~~>
            = "var" KwEnd PatName ;
PatName     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
// A list pattern ('[1, _, 3]', '[1, ..]', '[.. var rest]'): a '..' element matches any
// run of elements, and may itself carry a pattern for the slice it covers.
ListPat     <~~ push(csPatList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ ListElem { "," ListElem } ] [ "," ] "]" [ PatBind ] ;
ListElem    = SlicePat | Pattern ;
SlicePat    <~~ push(csPatSlice(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ".." [ Pattern ] ;
PosPatT     <~~ push(csPatPos(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatType "(" Pattern { "," Pattern } ")" [ PatBind ] ;
PropPat     <~~ push(csPatProp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ PatType ] "{" [ PropSub { "," PropSub } ] [ "," ] "}" [ PatBind ] ;
PropSub     <~~ push(csPatSub(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatName ":" !":" Pattern ;
PosPat      <~~ push(csPatPos(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" Pattern "," Pattern { "," Pattern } ")" [ PatBind ] ;
ParenPat    = "(" Pattern ")" ;
TypePat     <~~ push(csPatType(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatType [ PatBind ] ;
ConstPat    <~~ push(csPatConst(pop())) ~~>
            = Shift ;
PatType     <~~ push({patty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
PatBind     <~~ push({patbind: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
KwAnd       = "and" KwEnd ;
KwOr        = "or" KwEnd ;
KwNot       = "not" KwEnd ;

Shift       <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<<" | ">>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;

AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;

MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

// 'await e': the async machinery is not modelled (an async member runs synchronously),
// so await is the identity on its operand - which is what a synchronously completed task
// means. It is a rule without a tag, so the operand's own thunk stands.
// PreIncDec comes before Neg, or '--x' reads as the double negation '-(-x)'.
UnaryExpr   = Await | PreIncDec | Cast | Neg | Not | BitNot | PostfixExpr ;
PreIncDec   <~~ push(makePreIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Target ;
Await       = "await" KwEnd UnaryExpr ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
BitNot      <~~ push(makeBitNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "~" UnaryExpr ;

// A cast is identity ((Type) expr yields expr). A primitive cast accepts any unary
// operand; a reference cast excludes a leading '-'/'+' so '(a) - b' stays subtraction.
Cast        = PrimCast | RefCast ;
PrimCast    <~~ push(makeCast(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = "(" CastPrim { "*" | "[" "]" } ")" UnaryExpr ;
CastPrim    <~~ push({castty: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = PrimType ;
RefCast     <~~ push(makeCast(pop())) ~~>
            = "(" RefType ")" UnaryNoPM ;
UnaryNoPM   = Not | PostfixExpr ;
RefType     = ( "string" KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | UserType ) { "*" | "[" "]" } ;

// 'new C().m()' and 'base.M().n()' continue into the member chain, so an object
// creation and a base call are SEEDS of the chain (CallSeed), not alternatives beside it.
PostfixExpr = IncDec | CallMember ;
IncDec      <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// new Point(1, 2) | new List<int>() | new Dictionary<K,V>() | new int[]{...} | new int[n]
// | new Foo{ X = 1 } (object initializer) | new Foo(a){ X = 1 } (ctor + initializer)
NewExpr     = NewAnon | NewImplArr | NewObjInit | NewArrLit | NewArrSize | NewObject ;
// An anonymous type ('new { X = 1, Y = 2 }'): a plain object with those members.
NewAnon     <~~ push(makeAnon(pop())) ~~>
            = KwNew ObjInit ;
// An object initializer without constructor parens: 'new Foo{ X = 1, Y = 2 }' runs the
// parameterless constructor, then assigns each named member. Tried before NewArrLit so a
// collection literal ('new List<int>{1, 2}', whose entries are plain expressions) still
// falls through to it.
NewObjInit  <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewName ObjInit ;
// The created type's name. It may be qualified ('new Outer.Inner()', 'new System.Text.X()');
// only the last segment is kept, which is the simple name every type is registered under.
NewName     <~~ push({cname: csSimpleName(up.in), targs: csTypeArgs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
// The array type suffix ('[]') is part of Type, so 'new int[]{...}' consumes it here.
NewArrLit   <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew Type "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
// 'new[] { 1, 2, 3 }': an implicitly typed array - no type name at all.
NewImplArr  <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew "[" { "," } "]" "{" [ ArrElem { "," ArrElem } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
// 'new A[1] { new A() }': a sized array MAY still carry an initializer, which wins.
NewArrSize  <~~ push(makeSizedArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew Type "[" ArrDim { "," ArrDim } "]" { "[" { "," } "]" } [ ArrInit ] ;
ArrDim      <~~ push({adim: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
ArrInit     <~~ push({ainit: pop()}) ~~>
            = ArrLit ;
NewObject   <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewName "(" ArgList ")" [ ObjInit | SkipBlock ] ;
// { X = expr, ... }: the trailing member initializer of an object creation.
ObjInit     <~~ push({objinit: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" [ MemberInit { "," MemberInit } ] [ "," ] "}" ;
// 'X = e' assigns the member; 'P1 = { X = 1 }' is a NESTED initializer, which assigns
// into the object the member already holds rather than replacing it; '[k] = e' is an
// indexed initializer.
MemberInit  = NestedInit | IndexedInit | PlainInit ;
PlainInit   <~~ push(makeMemberInit(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" Expression ;
NestedInit  <~~ push(makeNestedInit(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" ObjInit ;
IndexedInit <~~ push(makeIndexedInit(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" "=" Expression ;

// base.M(args): the method search starts at the base class of the class the call
// appears in (curClassName is set while the class body is being built).
BaseCall    <~~ push(makeBaseCall(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwBase "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" ArgList ")" ;

// A primary followed by method calls, delegate calls, field reads and indexing. A bare
// '(args)' suffix invokes a delegate / lambda value (a closure call).
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CallSeed { MethodSfx | CallSfx | FieldSfx | IndexSfx
                      | NCMethodSfx | NCFieldSfx | NCIndexSfx | NFSfx | WithSfx } ;
// 'r with { Y = 9 }': a copy of the record with those members replaced.
WithSfx     <~~ push({kind: "with", winits: pop()}) ~~>
            = KwWith ObjInit ;
KwWith      = "with" KwEnd ;
// The null-forgiving operator 'e!': a compile-time assertion in C#, identity here. The
// '=' lookahead keeps 'a != b' out.
NFSfx       <~~ push({kind: "nfg"}) ~~>
            = "!" !"=" ;
// The head of a member chain. A bare 'M(args)' call inside a class body means
// 'this.M(args)' (or the class' own static M) when the enclosing class declares an M;
// otherwise it is an ordinary call of whatever the name holds (a lambda, a delegate).
// typeof / nameof / sizeof / default look exactly like a bare call, so they are matched
// before SelfCall.
CallSeed    = KwExpr | NewExpr | BaseCall | SelfCall | Primary | VarSeed ;
// A bare name in SEED position. It pushes its own name beside its thunk, so the member
// chain above it knows which variable it started at - which is what a call of a HIDDEN
// method ('public new string Tag()') needs, since C# resolves that one by the DECLARED
// type of the receiver rather than by the object's own class. Listed after Primary so
// 'true' / 'false' / 'null' are still keywords rather than identifiers.
VarSeed     <~~ push({seedvar: up.in, seedt: makeVarRef(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Id ;
KwExpr      = TypeofExpr | NameofExpr | SizeofExpr | DefaultExpr ;
SelfCall    <~~ push(makeSelfCall(popName(), takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" ArgList ")" ;

MethodSfx   <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] "(" ArgList ")" ;
CallSfx     <~~ push({kind:"call", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" ArgList ")" ;
FieldSfx    = "." Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;

// A call's argument list. A NAMED argument ('S22Vol(1, d: 5)') keeps its parameter name
// beside its thunk; csFixArgs turns the mixed list back into a positional one using the
// parameter names the declaration registered (csParamNames), and fills the skipped slots
// with the 'omitted' marker (undefined) that the parameter binder answers with the
// parameter's default. 'ref' / 'out' / 'in' at a call site are parsed and dropped.
ArgList     = [ Arg { "," Arg } ] ;
Arg         = NamedArg | PlainArg ;
NamedArg    <~~ push(csNamedArg(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ArgName ":" !":" [ ArgRefMod ] Expression ;
ArgName     <~~ push({argname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
PlainArg    = [ ArgRefMod ] Expression ;
ArgRefMod   = ( "ref" | "out" | "in" ) KwEnd ;

// A multi-dimensional index ('b[0, 0]') is accepted; the model has only flat arrays,
// so the further subscripts are evaluated (their calls stay visible) and dropped.
IndexSfx    <~~ push(csIndexSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" IdxArg { "," IdxArg } "]" ;
// An index argument: a plain index, an index counted from the END ('^1'), or a range
// ('1..3', '1..', '..3', '..'), which slices. It is written as one optional index
// followed by an optional '..' tail, so an ordinary index is still parsed exactly once
// (a 'range | index' choice would parse every index twice, which nests exponentially).
IdxArg      = [ IdxOne ] [ IdxTail ] ;
IdxOne      = FromEnd | Expression ;
FromEnd     <~~ push({fromEnd: pop()}) ~~>
            = "^" Expression ;
IdxTail     <~~ push(csIdxTail(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ".." [ IdxOne ] ;

// Null-conditional access ('a?.b', 'a?.m(...)', 'a?[i]'): when the receiver is null the
// WHOLE remaining chain is skipped and the value is null. The '?' must be followed by '.'
// or '[' so the ternary '?' is never captured here.
NCMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "mcall", nullsafe: true, name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "?" "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" ArgList ")" ;
NCFieldSfx  = "?" "." Id <~~ push({kind: "field", nullsafe: true, name: up.in}) ~~> ;
NCIndexSfx  <~~ push({kind: "index", nullsafe: true, idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "?" "[" Expression "]" ;

// A collection expression ('int[] xs = [1, 2, 3];', 'List<int> ys = [10, .. xs, 20];').
// Arrays and List<T> share one representation here, so both spell out the same value; a
// '..' spread element splices the elements of its operand in place.
CollExpr    <~~ push(makeCollection(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ CollElem { "," CollElem } ] [ "," ] "]" ;
CollElem    = SpreadElem | Expression ;
SpreadElem  <~~ push({spread: pop()}) ~~>
            = ".." Expression ;

Primary     = CollExpr | Lambda | AnonMethod | LinqQuery | "(" Expression ")" | TupleExpr
            | RawStr | VerbatimStr | InterpStr | DqString | CharLit
            | CsNum | IntLit | True | False | Null ;

// A tuple literal ('(1, "one")', '(lo: 3, hi: 9)'). It is listed AFTER the parenthesized
// expression, so an ordinary '(e)' is parsed exactly once: only a form that carries a
// top-level comma reaches this rule at all.
TupleExpr   <~~ push(makeTuple(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" TupItem "," TupItem { "," TupItem } ")" ;
TupItem     = TupNamed | TupPlain ;
TupNamed    <~~ push(csTupItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TupName ":" !":" Expression ;
TupName     <~~ push({tupname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Id ;
TupPlain    <~~ push(csTupItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Expression ;

// typeof(T) yields a small type token (a .Name / .FullName carrying object, never null);
// nameof(x) is the last segment of its argument, resolved at parse time; sizeof(T) is the
// size of a primitive; default(T) is T's default value (0 / false / the null char / null,
// and a zeroed instance for a struct). A bare 'default' outside a declaration is null.
TypeofExpr  <~~ push(makeTypeof(pop())) ~~>
            = "typeof" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
NameofExpr  <~~ push(makeNameof(pop())) ~~>
            = "nameof" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
SizeofExpr  <~~ push(makeSizeof(pop())) ~~>
            = "sizeof" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
DefaultExpr = DefaultOf | DefaultBare ;
DefaultOf   <~~ push(makeDefaultOf(pop())) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd "(" TypeArg ")" ;
DefaultBare <~~ push(makeDefaultOf("")) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd ;
TypeArg     <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Type ;

// A char literal 'x' (escapes included). C#'s char is an integral type that RENDERS
// as its glyph, so the value is a real char (a boxed code): it does arithmetic and
// compares as its code, and prints as the character. Reuses the balanced SkipChar shape.
CharLit     <~~ push(makeCharLit(up.in)) ~~> = SkipChar ;

// A raw string literal (triple-quoted): no escapes at all, and one or two quotes may
// appear inside it. Listed before the plain string so the opening triple quote is never
// read as the empty string followed by junk. A leading newline (the multi-line form) is stripped together
// with the indentation the closing delimiter sits at - see csRaw.
RawStr      <~~ push(makeConst(csRaw(up.in))) ~~>
            = '"' '"' '"' :whitespace() { RawQ2 | RawQ1 | RawPlain } '"' '"' '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
RawQ2       = '"' '"' RawPlain ;
RawQ1       = '"' RawPlain ;
RawPlain    = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// Verbatim strings. '@"..."' takes every byte literally (including newlines and
// backslashes); '""' is the escaped quote. '$@"..."' / '@$"..."' is the same text with
// '{expr}' interpolation holes, so it shares the interpolation productions.
VerbatimStr = VerbInterp | VerbPlain ;
VerbInterp  <~~ push(makeTemplate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = VerbIntro '"' :whitespace() { IsBrace | IsExpr | VbChunk } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
VerbIntro   = "$@" | "@$" ;
VbChunk     <~~ push({s: csVbText(up.in)}) ~~>
            = VbTxCh { VbTxCh } ;
VbTxCh      = VbDQuote | VbPlain ;
VbPlain     = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
VerbPlain   <~~ push(makeConst(csVerb(up.in))) ~~>
            = "@" '"' :whitespace() { VbDQuote | VbChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
VbDQuote    = '"' '"' ;
VbChar      = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// C# numeric literals beyond the plain decimal integer that IntLit already handles: hex
// (0x), binary (0b), reals (a '.' fraction, an exponent or an f/d/m suffix), and decimals
// with '_' digit groups or an integer u/l suffix. The value is computed by csNum.
CsNum       <~~ push(makeConst(csNum(up.in))) ~~>
            = CsHex | CsBin | CsDot | CsReal | CsDecU | CsDecS ;
CsHex       = ( "0x" | "0X" ) :whitespace() CsHexD { CsHexD | "_" } [ CsISfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsBin       = ( "0b" | "0B" ) :whitespace() CsBit { CsBit | "_" } [ CsISfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A leading-dot real ('.5'): only a digit may follow the dot, so a member access
// ('a.b') is never taken for a number.
CsDot       = "." :whitespace() "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ CsExp ] [ CsRSfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsReal      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" | "_" } CsRealT :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsDecU      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } "_" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ CsISfx ] :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsDecS      = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } CsISfx :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CsRealT     = "." "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } [ CsExp ] [ CsRSfx ] | CsExp [ CsRSfx ] | CsRSfx ;
CsExp       = ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
CsHexD      = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
CsBit       = "0" | "1" ;
CsISfx      = ( "u" | "U" | "l" | "L" ) [ "u" | "U" | "l" | "L" ] ;
CsRSfx      = "f" | "F" | "d" | "D" | "m" | "M" ;

// A lambda ('x => e', '(a, b) => { ... }') is a real closure value (a Func / Action
// delegate): it captures the current scope chain and is invoked at a call site (CallSfx).
Lambda      <~~ push(makeLambda(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LambdaMod ] LambdaHead "=>" LambdaBody ;
// 'static x => ...' / 'async x => ...': parsed and dropped (capture is not restricted).
LambdaMod   = ( "static" | "async" ) KwEnd ;
LambdaHead  = LamParenHead | LamBareHead ;
LamParenHead <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ LamParam { "," LamParam } ] ")" ;
LamBareHead <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = LamBareParam ;
// A lambda parameter is a full parameter (type, 'params', a default value) or a bare
// name. The full form is an ordered alternative so a bare name is not swallowed by a
// greedy optional type.
LamParam    = Param | LamBareParam ;
LamBareParam <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = Id ;
